$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: safekeys/dev-key-custody
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-28"
summary: >-
  Development-stack key custody: the KEK must not be readable by the same OS user the coding agent
  runs as. The default dev flow stores the KEK in the macOS Keychain (ACL restricted to the sidecar
  binary) or a containerised OpenBao dev instance on Linux; a plaintext file survives only behind
  SAFEKEYS_ALLOW_INSECURE_KEYSTORE=1 with a loud startup warning and a permission check, and a
  documented separate-user mode runs the sidecar as a dedicated user with peer-credential checks on
  the socket.
$system: safekeys/system
$service: safekeys/sidecar
intent: >-
  The dev stack currently stores the KEK in a 0600 file owned by the same OS user as any coding
  agent, so an agent with file access can read it and the deployment's separate-user promise does
  not exist locally. The default dev flow must keep the KEK out of an agent-readable file — Keychain
  on macOS, the containerised dev OpenBao on Linux — with a plaintext file available only behind an
  explicit, loudly-warned opt-in, and a documented separate-user mode for hosts where the stronger
  boundary matters.
status: built
flows:
  - id: dev-kek-in-keychain
    name: Dev KEK lives in the macOS Keychain
    steps:
      - action: setup
        id: s1
        target: >-
          scripts/dev-up.sh creates or reuses a Keychain item whose ACL grants only the sidecar
          binary.
      - action: receive
        id: s2
        target: >-
          The sidecar reads the KEK from the Keychain at startup; no plaintext KEK file exists on
          disk.
      - action: observe
        id: s3
        target: >-
          A same-user process other than the sidecar reading the item triggers a user-visible ACL
          prompt or denial.
      - action: observe
        id: s4
        target: .dev/ contains no KEK file and the sidecar resolves normally.
  - id: dev-kek-in-openbao
    name: Dev KEK lives in the dev OpenBao instance
    steps:
      - action: setup
        id: s1
        target: The dev Compose stack runs OpenBao in a container under its own user, as it already does.
      - action: receive
        id: s2
        target: >-
          The sidecar uses Vault transit by default on Linux; the KEK never exists as a file the
          agent user can read.
      - action: observe
        id: s3
        target: No KEK file appears in .dev/ or the home directory.
  - id: insecure-file-opt-in
    name: Plaintext key file is an explicit, loud opt-in
    steps:
      - action: receive
        id: s1
        target: SAFEKEYS_ALLOW_INSECURE_KEYSTORE=1 is the only way the local file keystore activates.
      - action: validate
        id: s2
        target: The sidecar refuses to start when the key file is group- or world-readable.
      - action: observe
        id: s3
        target: Startup prints a loud warning that the mode is not for real secrets.
      - action: observe
        id: s4
        target: Documentation marks the mode as not for real secrets.
  - id: separate-user-local-mode
    name: Separate-user local mode with peer-credential checks
    steps:
      - action: setup
        id: s1
        target: >-
          A documented script creates a dedicated sidecar user and an agent group; the sidecar runs
          as the dedicated user.
      - action: validate
        id: s2
        target: >-
          The sidecar checks the peer credential (uid/gid) of every socket connection, not only
          filesystem permissions.
      - action: observe
        id: s3
        target: >-
          An agent in the group resolves; a process outside the group is denied even if socket
          permissions change.
contracts:
  - description: >-
      The default development flow must not leave a key-encryption key readable by the same OS user
      the coding agent runs as.
    id: default-dev-no-readable-kek
    must_have:
      - >-
        The default dev flow leaves no KEK file readable by the agent user in the repo or home
        directory
      - macOS stores the dev KEK in the Keychain with access restricted to the sidecar binary
      - >-
        Linux stores the dev KEK in a containerised OpenBao dev instance running as its own user,
        never a plaintext file
      - The plaintext key-file path is removed from the default dev flow
  - description: The plaintext key-file mode is opt-in, loud, and refuses unsafe permissions.
    id: insecure-file-strict
    must_have:
      - File mode requires SAFEKEYS_ALLOW_INSECURE_KEYSTORE=1 explicitly
      - Startup prints a loud warning naming the mode not for real secrets when file mode is active
      - The sidecar refuses to start with a key file that is group- or world-readable
      - Documentation marks file mode as not for real secrets
  - description: A documented local mode reproduces the deployment's user separation on a developer machine.
    id: separate-user-socket
    must_have:
      - A documented mode runs the sidecar as a dedicated user with agents in a shared group
      - The sidecar verifies socket peer credentials on every connection
      - A process outside the allowed group is denied even if filesystem permissions change
      - Key material and .dev/ state are gitignored and excluded from image build contexts
tests:
  - contracts:
      - default-dev-no-readable-kek
    expect:
      - assertion: no KEK file readable by the agent user exists at .dev/kek or ~/.safekeys/kek
      - assertion: the sidecar resolves a token successfully
    flow: dev-kek-in-keychain
    id: default-dev-leaves-no-kek-file
    setup:
      command: scripts/dev-up.sh
      platform: macos
  - contracts:
      - insecure-file-strict
    expect:
      - assertion: the sidecar exits with an error and serves nothing
    flow: insecure-file-opt-in
    id: insecure-file-refuses-loose-perms
    setup:
      keyfile_mode: "0644"
  - contracts:
      - insecure-file-strict
    expect:
      - assertion: the startup log contains the not-for-real-secrets warning
      - assertion: resolution works
    flow: insecure-file-opt-in
    id: insecure-file-warns-loudly
    setup:
      keyfile_mode: "0600"
      opt_in: true
  - contracts:
      - separate-user-socket
    expect:
      - assertion: the agent user in the group can connect and resolve
      - assertion: a user outside the group is denied even if socket permissions are relaxed
    flow: separate-user-local-mode
    id: separate-user-denies-outsider
    setup:
      agent_user: in-group
      other_user: out-group
decisions:
  - id: keep-insecure-keystore-name
    decision: >-
      Keep SAFEKEYS_ALLOW_INSECURE_KEYSTORE as the file-mode opt-in rather than renaming to
      SAFEKEYS_DEV_INSECURE_KEYFILE.
    rationale: >-
      The variable already exists, is documented, and is asserted by the production refuse test.
      Renaming it would churn every reference for no security gain; the semantics being added
      (permission check, loud warning) do not require a new name.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-28"
  - id: keychain-is-a-speed-bump-not-a-boundary
    decision: Document the macOS Keychain ACL as raising the bar, not as an OS boundary.
    rationale: >-
      A same-user process can still trigger the ACL prompt and a user can approve it. The honest
      claim is that an agent cannot silently read the KEK; the strong local boundary is the
      separate-user mode, which mirrors the deployment threat model.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-28"
  - id: openbao-dev-is-the-linux-default
    decision: Make the containerised dev OpenBao the default Linux dev keystore instead of the local file.
    rationale: >-
      The dev Compose stack already runs OpenBao under its own container user; pointing the sidecar
      at Vault transit costs no new infrastructure and removes the plaintext file from the default
      path entirely.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-28"
implementation:
  primary: pkg/keystore/keychain_darwin.go
  test_code: pkg/keystore/keystore_test.go
  test_code_status: manual
see_also:
  - safekeys/envelope-encryption
  - safekeys/deployment-packaging
  - safekeys/sidecar-resolution
